<main>
<p class="l-kicker">Plan &middot; Payments and Platform <span class="l-tag l-muted">Draft</span></p>
<h1>Stored cards move to the new provider by 15 December</h1>
<p class="l-lede">Every stored card is copied to the new provider's vault and read from there by 15 December 2026, so
the old contract can end on 31 December without a customer entering a card again.</p>
<p class="l-meta">PAY-160 &middot; canopy worker, for Sam Okafor, 27 October 2026</p>
<dl>
<dt>Goal</dt><dd>Every stored card charges through the new vault, with no change a customer sees.</dd>
<dt>Not a goal</dt><dd>Moving payouts, which stay with the old provider until March.</dd>
<dt>Done by</dt><dd>15 December, two weeks before the old contract ends.</dd>
</dl>
<h2>The steps</h2>
<ol class="l-steps">
<li><b>New cards go to both vaults.</b> Every card saved since 20 October is written to the old vault and the new
one. <span class="l-tag l-good">Done</span></li>
<li><b>Backfill the 1.2 million stored cards.</b> The old provider exports them encrypted to the new one's key; we
match each to its customer by fingerprint. <span class="l-tag">Next</span></li>
<li><b>Charge from the new vault.</b> Behind a flag, 1% of charges first, then all of them over a week.</li>
<li><b>Stop writing to the old vault.</b> Once a week of charges has matched, and the finance team signs off.</li>
</ol>
<h2>Risks</h2>
<div class="l-callout l-risk">The export needs the old provider's help, and they have not yet given us a date. If it
slips past 20 November, we ask for a month's extension of the old contract.</div>
<p>A card that fails to match by fingerprint stays in the old vault, and its customer is asked for it again at their
next purchase.</p>
<p>Charges from the new vault could decline more often while its fraud model learns our traffic.</p>
<h2>Open questions</h2>
<ul>
<li>Does finance need a reconciliation report for each step, or only at the end?</li>
<li>Who at the old provider owns the export?</li>
</ul>
<footer>
<p>Sources: the new provider's migration guide, and the old contract's termination terms.</p>
</footer>
</main>
